%20Nottinghamshire%20%28geograph%201061761%29.jpg","article":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall","cat":"Thrumpton Hall","stories":[{"layer":"barton","text":"The Powdrells, a Roman Catholic family, lost their house here after the Babington Plot; part of it survives inside the hall, with a priest hole. The Pigots' rose-brick house around it was largely complete by 1617.","source":{"title":"Thrumpton Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"}},{"layer":"barton","text":"The hall's Jacobean cantilevered staircase was carved from the estate's own timber, under John Webb, a pupil of Inigo Jones.","source":{"title":"Thrumpton Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"}},{"layer":"barton","text":"The hall came to the Byrons when George Byron, 8th Baron Byron, married Lucy Wescomb in 1843, and they kept it for about a hundred years. Frederick, the 10th Baron, was the village's vicar and from 1917 its squire as well: its \"squarson\".","source":{"title":"Thrumpton Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"}},{"layer":"barton","text":"George Fitzroy Seymour owned the estate from 1949 to 1994, and Miranda Seymour has owned it since.","source":{"title":"Thrumpton Hall","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall","kind":"Wikipedia"}}],"sum":"Thrumpton Hall is an English country house in the village of Thrumpton near Nottingham. It operated as a wedding venue until November 2020.","sumSrc":{"title":"Thrumpton Hall","kind":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thrumpton_Hall"},"council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.24758,52.87641]},"19371":{"h":10,"osm":"way/275946691","council":"Rushcliffe Borough Council","xy":[-1.24722,52.87663]},"19372":{"y":1820,"f":"century","src":"Historic England (via Wikipedia)","dt":"Early 19th century","h":10,"osm":"way/275931529","kind":"barn","listEntry":1242430,"listUrl":"https://historicengland.org.uk/listin